  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 329 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nToday... \n\nMuch of weather over the last 36 hours has been dominated \nby a large, broad area of high pressure over the SE CONUS. A \ncombination of a strong subgeostrophic upper jet and weak low \npressure over the Atlantic has subdued the amplitude and strength of \nridge, with the upper jet now dipping into portions of the Ohio \nValley. \n\nIn the lower levels, a closed low and associated SW jet is exiting \nto the NE. This jet pushed large amounts of moisture into the \nregion, of which has created a broad area of instability over \ncentral Indiana. Latest soundings indicate SBCAPE values of around \n1500 J/kg, with 2500 J/kg expected by late this afternoon. With \nconvective temperatures around 90 degrees, a broad cirrus shield, \nand a modest low level warm layer, convection is likely going to be \nconfined to weakly forced areas along residual outflow boundaries. \n\nThe severe threat today will be rather limited with bulk shear \nvalues less than 20kt, but lapse rates in the mid to upper levels \nare steep enough for a few isolated strong cores to develop. In \nthese cases, a brief area of enhanced winds (likely 40-50MPH) would \nbe possible. \n\n\nTonight...\n\nAs we head into the overnight period, a weak positively tilted \ntrough will approach from the NW. As this interacts with the high \npressure to the south, confluence will promote the development of a \nnocturnal LLJ. This in combination with a modest low level boundary \nand a saturated thermal profile will likely lead to a narrow (50-\n100mi) corridor of persistent thunderstorms. Model guidance is still \nwidely varied on placement of these thunderstorms, with the latest \nHREF LPMM highlighting a corridor of 3-5\" over E/NE Indiana. \nConfidence is not high on if NE central will be within this narrow \ncorridor, but the margin of error is low enough, and the impact \npotential is high enough that a Flood Watch has been issued for far \nN/NE central Indiana from midnight tonight until 2PM EDT tomorrow \nencompassing the greatest timing concern of 4AM to 11AM EDT. \n\nSevere weather a distant secondary concern overnight, but the mid \nlevel lapse rates due remain steep enough for a few stronger cores \nto be embedded within the persistent thunderstorm threat. For this \nreason, isolated gusts of 40-50 MPH will be possible overnight.\n\nThe LLJ will weaken with diurnal heating on Thursday, of which \nshould dampen convective initiation late Thursday morning into the \nearly afternoon. This should lead to a lull in thunderstorms for a \nportion of tomorrow before diurnal convection returns along remnant \nboundaries in the late afternoon and evening. \n\nFriday and beyond...\n\nThe upper high will continue to gradually retreat southwest. The \nsurface front will sink far enough south initially to keep PoPs low \non Friday, but it will return north again on Saturday as an upper \nwave interacts with the front. This will bring more rain chances, \nmainly north. \n\nAfter a brief reprieve from heat for many areas Friday, hot \nconditions return on Saturday as the front moves back north.\n\nSunday into next week, larger upper waves moving through will keep \nchances for rain around. Cooler air will filter back into the area, \nwith highs by Tuesday in the upper 70s to middle 80s.",
